<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad al-Bukhari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/810">810</a>–<a href="/years/870">870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as al-Bukhari, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad al-Bukhari was a 9th-century Muslim muhaddith from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, widely regarded as the most important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> scholar in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>. He began studying hadith at the age of ten, travelled throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and is said to have examined more than 200,000 hadiths. His principal collection, the Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī, compiled in 846, selected 7563 narrations including repetitions and arranged them by subject in ninety-seven books. With the Ṣaḥīḥ of his student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-ibn-al-hajjaj">Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj</a> it is regarded by Sunnis as the principal compilation of reliable hadith. Near the end of his life he was exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and moved to Khartank, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Bukhari was born after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayer</a> on 21 July 810, in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in Greater Khorasan, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. His father, Ismail ibn Ibrahim, was a scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and a student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas">Malik ibn Anas</a>, and died while his son was an infant. The family's nisba, al-Ju'fi, came from his great-grandfather al-Mughirah, who settled in Bukhara after accepting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> at the hands of the governor Yaman al-Ju'fi and became his mawla.</p><p>He memorised the works of Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak while still a child. At sixteen he made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> with his elder brother and his widowed mother, stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> for two years and then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, where he wrote a book on the companions of Muhammad and the tabi'un and a historical work, al-Tarikh al-Kabir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Al-Bukhari travelled to most of the important centres of Islamic learning of his time, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. His teachers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hanbal">Ahmad ibn Hanbal</a>, Ali ibn al-Madini, Yahya ibn Ma'in and Ishaq ibn Rahwayh. His other works include al-Adab al-Mufrad on ethics and manners, Kitab al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guna-people">Kuna</a> on patronymics, a small book on weak narrators and an abridged history arranged by generations and years.</p><p>His collection, al-Jāmiʿ al-Ṣaḥīḥ, was published first at Firabr after he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and again at Nasaf after his expulsion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> 256 AH. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholars regard it as second only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> in authenticity, and it is one of the six collections called the Kutub al-Sittah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Bukhari reportedly said that his recitation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> was created, meaning the human act of reading and not God's word. Prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> scholars received the statement badly and he was driven out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>. Al-Dhahabi and Taj al-Din al-Subki attributed his expulsion to the jealousy of certain scholars there. He wrote the treatise Khalq Afʿāl al-ʿIbād in reply, setting out that the Quran is God's uncreated speech while human actions are created, and citing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hanbal">Ahmad ibn Hanbal</a>.</p><p>He took refuge at Khartank, a village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, and died there on Friday, 1 September 870. His tomb lies in the Imam Bukhari Mausoleum in Hartang, 25 kilometres from Samarkand, which was restored in 1998 after centuries of neglect. The complex has a mosque, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and a library, and the tombstone at ground level is a cenotaph, the grave itself lying in a crypt below.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad al-Bukhari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
